export default "Breastfeeding breaks every assumption period trackers rely on. Standard cycle prediction algorithms need recent period data to work. During lactational amenorrhea, the suppression of ovulation caused by frequent nursing, there is no recent period data. Some people go 6 months without a period. Some go 18 months. The app needs to handle that gap without generating false alarms.\n\n## What Breastfeeding Parents Need From a Period Tracker\n\nThree things matter: tolerance for long gaps between periods, useful logging during the waiting period, and honest predictions when cycles start returning. Breastfeeding-specific features like nursing session logs are a bonus, but that is a different product category that occasionally overlaps.\n\nThe hormonal reality is straightforward. Prolactin, the hormone that drives milk production, suppresses GnRH pulsatility. This delays ovulation. As nursing frequency decreases through night weaning, introducing solids, or supplementing with formula, prolactin drops and ovulation returns. Your first postpartum period follows ovulation by roughly two weeks, which means you can become fertile before you see any bleeding.\n\n## Floriva: Best for Private, Patient Tracking\n\nFloriva handles the core problem well. It does not generate predictions when there is no data to predict from. During the months between birth and period return, it functions as a symptom and mood logger without guessing when your next period will arrive. When bleeding does return, it builds prediction data from actual observations rather than statistical averages.\n\nThe privacy architecture matters here specifically. Postpartum health data, including mood logs, bleeding patterns, and hormonal symptom records, is sensitive. Floriva keeps core records on-device and has no readable central cycle database, which means no employer wellness program can receive your postpartum recovery data from Floriva. For breastfeeding parents returning to work, this is a concrete concern.\n\nThe gap is breastfeeding-specific features. There is no nursing session tracker, no pumping log, no supply notes. If you want that integrated with your period tracker, Ovia is the better choice. Floriva is a period tracker that handles postpartum well, not a postpartum app that includes period tracking.\n\n## Ovia: Best for Integrated Breastfeeding Logging\n\nOvia is the only app in this list built around the full parenting journey: pregnancy, postpartum, and baby tracking in one product. The breastfeeding logging (session times, side tracking, pumping volumes) lives alongside period tracking, so you can see the relationship between nursing patterns and cycle return in one interface.\n\nThe tradeoff is data architecture. Ovia's business model includes partnerships with employer wellness programs. Your breastfeeding frequency data and postpartum mood logs exist on Ovia's servers and contribute to aggregate datasets. The company states data is de-identified, but the collection itself is broader than what a privacy-focused user might want.\n\n## The Rest of the Field\n\n**Flo** has a postpartum mode that technically delays predictions, but it reengages prediction algorithms earlier than cycle data justifies. The FTC settlement over sharing health data with Facebook and Google is especially relevant for postpartum users logging sensitive recovery data.\n\n**Clue** handles irregular data gracefully by design. It does not generate confident predictions from insufficient data. No breastfeeding features, but a clean tracker for the period-return-waiting phase.\n\n**Glow** offers a postpartum mode with baby tracking, but the interface is cluttered with social features and ads. Data privacy practices have faced scrutiny.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nThree criteria drove rankings: (1) how well the app tolerates months without period data, (2) whether it provides useful non-prediction features during lactational amenorrhea, and (3) data privacy architecture. Breastfeeding-specific features were a bonus but not a requirement. Most breastfeeding parents already use a dedicated nursing tracker.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nPostpartum health data is among the most sensitive categories period trackers collect. Mood logs during a time when postpartum depression screening is active. Bleeding patterns that might indicate complications. Hormonal symptom records that map to recovery timelines. The question is not whether this data is valuable. The question is who holds it and what they do with it. On-device storage removes that question entirely. Server-based apps require you to trust their current and future data practices with information generated during one of the most vulnerable periods of your life.";
